## What I made

**`temperature.html`** — a self-contained, single-file interactive chart of NASA GISS global temperature anomalies (1880–2025), regenerated by **`build_chart.py`** from live data I fetched off `data.giss.nasa.gov`.

It's built to the dataviz skill's method for a single-series change-over-time chart — one categorical hue, hairline recessive grid, zero-baseline from the 1951–80 average, all marks/specs validated against the palette script in both light and dark.

**The data it tells:** anomalies rose from **−0.25°C in 1880** to **+1.26°C in 2025** — a **+0.09°C/decade** trend — with **2024** the warmest year on record at **+1.43°C**.

**Included:**
- SVG line + area wash with a dashed linear-trend overlay and direct end-label
- Crosshair + tooltip hover (verified: shows "+1.24°C · 2020 vs 1951–80 avg"), plus keyboard focus support
- Light/dark mode toggle (respects the OS setting, validated palettes for both)
- A full 146-row data table (deliberately un-gated, per the skill's accessibility rule)
- A stat-tile hero reading, since the chart has a headline number

**Verified, not assumed:** I installed Chromium headlessly and confirmed via rendered output — 146 points mapped, no text overflow, hover tooltip fires, theme toggle works, zero console errors, and light/dark actually differ.

